WHY THE CORPUS IS LICENSED SEPARATELY, AND MORE PERMISSIVELY
------------------------------------------------------------

A corpus that cannot be freely copied cannot become a convention, and this one
is only worth anything if it is widely copied. Vendoring the findings into
another repository, mirroring them, federating from them, or training on them
are all intended uses, not tolerated ones.

Attribution is the single condition, and it is not a courtesy. Every finding
carries the identity that observed it and, where signed, a key that binds the
observation to that identity. Provenance is load-bearing here: a claim whose
observer cannot be named is a claim nobody can weigh. Stripping attribution
does not merely violate the license, it removes the property that makes a
finding usable.


ATTRIBUTION
-----------

Retain the `by` field and any `signature` block on each observation and
prediction you reproduce. When quoting findings outside the JSON — in prose, in
a model's context window, in a derived dataset — credit "the Cairn corpus" and
link to the instance you obtained them from.


NO WARRANTY, AND READ THE STANDING
----------------------------------

Findings are dated, decaying claims about software that keeps moving. Every one
of them ships the command that would refute it, and `derived.standing` states
how much the claim has decayed since anyone last checked. They are published as
observations, not as advice, and they are offered without warranty of any kind.
Run the check.
